codex-plugin: let a message be queued onto a thread while its turn is streaming

Target: the codex@openai-codex Claude Code plugin (any 1.0.x; applied with
context matching, not line numbers, so it survives version bumps).

Why:
  A Codex job launched through the companion runs as an app-server thread
  behind a per-cwd broker. While a turn streams, the broker answers every
  request from another socket with -32001 "Shared Codex broker is busy",
  with exactly one exception: turn/interrupt.

  That leaves interrupt as the only way to say anything to a running job, and
  interrupt is destructive - it stops the turn wherever it happens to be,
  which for a large multi-file edit means a half-applied change. The
  non-destructive equivalent already exists in the app-server as
  thread/queue/add: the message waits and the agent picks it up when it
  finishes the turn it is running, which is how a human redirects an agent.

  Two things block it: the broker does not forward the method during an
  active stream, and the plugin's client declares experimentalApi: false, so
  the server rejects thread/queue/add outright.

What this changes:
  1. scripts/lib/app-server.mjs - declare experimentalApi: true in the default
     client capabilities.
  2. scripts/app-server-broker.mjs - forward thread/queue/add alongside
     turn/interrupt while another socket owns the stream.

Nothing else is touched; job state keying, streaming and interrupt behaviour
are unchanged. Reverse with: crew-codex patch --revert

--- a/scripts/lib/app-server.mjs
+++ b/scripts/lib/app-server.mjs
@@ -31,7 +31,7 @@
 
 /** @type {InitializeCapabilities} */
 const DEFAULT_CAPABILITIES = {
-  experimentalApi: false,
+  experimentalApi: true,
   requestAttestation: false,
   optOutNotificationMethods: [
     "item/agentMessage/delta",
--- a/scripts/app-server-broker.mjs
+++ b/scripts/app-server-broker.mjs
@@ -33,8 +33,21 @@
   socket.write(`${JSON.stringify(message)}\n`);
 }

-function isInterruptRequest(message) {
-  return message?.method === "turn/interrupt";
+// Requests that stay safe to forward while another socket owns the stream.
+// turn/interrupt was already allowed and is the destructive one: it stops the
+// turn wherever it stands. The other two are not destructive. turn/steer
+// interjects a message into the running turn, which the model reads at its
+// next step once the in-flight tool call has finished, and thread/queue/add
+// leaves a message the agent picks up after the turn completes. Refusing them
+// leaves interrupt as the only way to say anything to a running job.
+const ACTIVE_STREAM_PASSTHROUGH_METHODS = new Set([
+  "turn/interrupt",
+  "turn/steer",
+  "thread/queue/add"
+]);
+
+function isActiveStreamPassthrough(message) {
+  return ACTIVE_STREAM_PASSTHROUGH_METHODS.has(message?.method);
 }
 
 function writePidFile(pidFile) {
@@ -168,7 +177,7 @@
         }
 
         const allowInterruptDuringActiveStream =
-          isInterruptRequest(message) && activeStreamSocket && activeStreamSocket !== socket && !activeRequestSocket;
+          isActiveStreamPassthrough(message) && activeStreamSocket && activeStreamSocket !== socket && !activeRequestSocket;
 
